From 89e288f237f9e8dcdac7ab2d99942978260786ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 14:07:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] EXIT was eight pixels out, and make ADD AN ADDRESS work both
 ways

The EXIT button was drawn by draw_box_icon(38, 208), which lands at
(324, 224), while every clickmap said (324, 216) - so the region you could
press sat eight pixels above the icon you could see, which is exactly what
"clicking exit is off" feels like. One pair of numbers now, used by the
drawing and by all four clickmaps, hard into the bottom-right corner of
the frame: 8-pixel frame, 16-pixel icon, 360x254 box, so (336, 230).
Sharing the numbers is the part that stops them drifting apart again.

ADD AN ADDRESS moves two characters left and a row down, and the panel's
vertical stack is retuned around it - eight rows of machines, then the
discovery line, the status line, ADD, and the version - each at its own
macro rather than as an offset from its neighbour, so moving one no longer
lands it on top of another.

The discovery line now shows on both machines whatever the list holds, not
only when it is empty, and carries this machine's session id so two
screenshots can be told apart. It reads out sent, failed, packets in and
beacons in, with the address the last one came from.

And the fix that may be the actual answer: the beacon now also goes
straight to every typed-in address, not only to the broadcast address.
Broadcast is the half that firewalls and wireless access points quietly
drop; a unicast datagram to a named machine is ordinary traffic and
usually gets through where a broadcast does not. So adding the other
machine's address on ONE side is now enough for both to see each other -
that side learns nothing new from it, but the far end hears from us and
lists us without anybody typing anything there.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
